---
// A "skip link": lets a keyboard or screen reader user jump past the site header and navigation
// straight to the page's content (WCAG 2.4.1, Bypass Blocks). It only works as the first thing in
// <body>, so BaseLayout renders it before <Header />. Its target, `#main`, is the <main> element
// in BaseLayout.
//
// Hidden until it receives focus, then shown in the page's top corner, so a sighted keyboard user
// can see where focus is (WCAG 2.4.7, Focus Visible). A mouse user never sees it.
---

<a href="#main" class="skip-link">Skip to the main content.</a>

<style>
  /* Wrapped in `@layer components`: Astro's scoped styles are otherwise unlayered, and unlayered
     CSS beats every layer. The wrapper keeps the cascade order from src/styles/index.css true. */
  @layer components {
    /* How it looks once focused: a small box over the top corner of the page, with the page's
       background so it stays readable over whatever is beneath it (the site-wide banner). The
       link colour and underline come from base.css. */
    .skip-link {
      position: absolute;
      inset-block-start: 0.5rem;
      inset-inline-start: 0.5rem;
      z-index: 1;
      padding: 0.5em 1em;
      border: 0.1rem solid var(--color-link);
      border-radius: 0.25rem;
      background-color: var(--color-bg);
      font-size: var(--fluid-type--1);
    }

    /* Hidden the rest of the time, in a way that keeps it in the accessibility tree and in the
       tab order. This is the same technique as the `.visually-hidden` utility, written out here
       on purpose: that class is in the `utilities` layer, which beats `components`, so a
       `:focus` rule here could never undo it. Scoping the hiding to `:not(:focus)` avoids
       needing to undo anything. */
    .skip-link:not(:focus) {
      clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
      overflow: hidden;
      width: 1px;
      height: auto;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      border: 0;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }
  }
</style>
